 tools="Python 3, NLTK (WordNet, Lesk), scikit-learn, TextBlob, numpy, pandas, matplotlib. Data: IMDB reviews of 280-700 words from the NLTK movie_reviews corpus.",
 theory=["Meaning analysis (semantics). A word can have several senses ('bank' has 18 noun and verb senses in WordNet). Word sense disambiguation (WSD) chooses the correct sense from the context; the Lesk algorithm picks the sense whose dictionary definition overlaps most with the context words. Sentence similarity can be lexical (Jaccard overlap of word sets, cosine between TF-IDF vectors) or semantic (WordNet path similarity of the words), so that 'movie' and 'film' count as similar.",
         "Text summarization produces a short version that keeps the important information. Extractive summarization selects the most important sentences of the document (abstractive summarization writes new sentences). Frequency-based scoring ranks a sentence by the normalised frequencies of its words. TF-IDF scoring ranks it by the sum of TF-IDF weights. TextRank builds a graph in which sentences are nodes and the edge weight is their cosine similarity, and runs PageRank, so the most 'central' sentences get the highest score. Lead-3 simply takes the first sentences, which is a strong baseline for news.",
         "Evaluation uses ROUGE (n-gram overlap between a summary and a reference). Because the corpus has no human summaries, ROUGE-1 F1 against the full review, keyword coverage and sentiment preservation (does the summary keep the polarity of the review) are used as proxies."],
